    feat: layer 10 grades the toolchain the repository kind needs, Go included — a node could print PASS with no Go toolchain for the fleet's largest repo class.
    
    Layer 10 graded a fixed set (python3+venv, git, curl, tar, sha256sum) with jq/rg/node
    WARN-only, so a node for a Go repo could certify itself green while unable to build: a
    census of 61 distinct fleet repo origins measures go 27, node 22, python 16, rust 5, and
    cf-node measured "go MISSING", "rg MISSING" behind PASS.
    
    The graded set is now derived from the project KIND, from evidence in a fixed order, and
    the row names which evidence it used:
    
      a) --kinds <csv> / NODE_KINDS (go,rust,node,python,generic), validated before any layer
         runs exactly like --layers: an empty or unknown element is a named FAIL kinds and
         exits 1, never a silent pass down the generic path;
      b) the $HOME/repo workspace when a clone is already present (go.mod, Cargo.toml,
         package.json, pyproject.toml|setup.py) — layer 10 runs before layer 40 clones, so a
         workspace is the strongest evidence a fresh node can carry;
      c) the generic fallback, which the layer now PRINTS as a fallback instead of silently
         grading the old fixed list — the bug this row exists to fix.
    
    Per kind: go grades the binary, its version read back, and the node's own GOPROXY
    resolving the pinned rsc.io/quote@v1.5.2 through a cold module cache under a 30 s bound,
    failing with the proxy URL and the error text; rust grades cargo --version read back;
    node grades node --version and is a FAIL (not the old WARN) once node is a declared or
    derived kind; python keeps its existing python3 + venv rows; jq and rg stay WARN-only
    helpers. Every new row prints the measured value, and the layer prints its own census
    command so the counts can be re-measured.
    
    Contradictions named (AGENTS.md rule 1): specs/NODE.md layer 10's cell said "python3 +
    venv, git, curl, tar, sha256sum; optionally jq, rg, node", and layer 00's cell ended with
    the CF-164 sentence that no shipped layer probes a Go module proxy and that GOPROXY /
    GOPRIVATE appear nowhere under scripts/. Both became false with this change, so both
    cells changed in this commit: layer 10 now documents the derived grading, and layer 00
    points at layer 10 for the Go proxy instead of claiming nobody measures one.
    
    Docs that had to move with the code (disclosed, fifth path): docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md. Its
    section-39 gate (docs/audits/cf198-section-mapping.sh) derives the numbered-section count
    and the mapping table from the tree, so a new tests/selftest.sh section cannot land
    without a plan row: it failed "arms_plan=51 arms_tree=52" until the plan-row for the new
    section and the 49-section / 52-arm prose (with the CF-218 governing row) landed. Both
    census gates are green after the edit: cf198 prints RESULT: OK (arms_plan=52
    arms_tree=52), cf099 --sections-only prints plan_sections=49 selftest_sections=49.
    
    Gates: bash -n scripts/project-node.sh; bash -n tests/selftest.sh; bash tests/selftest.sh
    -> SELFTEST: PASS, exit 0, 316 PASS rows, with the new section's eight arms green: RED
    (a go.mod tree without go -> exit 1 and exactly one FAIL tool:go row, no WARN), GREEN
    (the same tree with a stub go that resolves the pinned module -> go rows PASS, exit 0),
    a refusing proxy -> FAIL go:proxy naming the proxy and the error, --kinds rust,node on a
    marker-less tree grades cargo and node and never go, a declared node kind with no node
    -> FAIL tool:node instead of the old WARN, the generic fallback naming itself, and a
    typo'd kind refused by name before any layer runs.
    
    Addresses CF-218.
    
